  "title": "Avoid redundant padding copies for single-sequence batches",
  "body": "## Problem\n\n`zero_pad_sequences` calls `F.pad` even when a batch contains one sequence. Zero-width padding creates a complete clone, and the following `cat` or `stack` allocates the final batch again. This repeated copy occurs in the default microbatch-size-one SFT collation path.\n\n## Change\n\nFor a single input, call the existing final `cat`/`stack` directly. The result still owns independent storage and retains the existing shape, layout and autograd behavior. The condition follows from the batch structure: one input cannot need padding to match another input. Multi-input batches keep the original padding loop.\n\n## Validation\n\n- Independent upstream base: `26494490bc237f6c3af62dab6117e6bbb0a78dba`.\n- Native CPU regression file: 78 passes and two allocation-budget failures before; 80 passes after. All original semantic controls pass.\n- Covers left/right padding, cat/stack, numeric and Boolean tensors, empty tensors, single/multiple sequences, noncontiguous and expanded views, channels-last layout, output storage independence, exact output strides and gradients.\n- Actual `SFTDataset` and `DataLoader` with a local tokenizer feed GPT2 through three SGD updates. Every parameter matches the existing collation algorithm for microbatch sizes 1 and 2 in SFT and pretraining modes.\n- `python -m compileall -q openrlhf examples tests` passes; the full `python -m pytest -q` run reports 283 passed and 2 skipped.\n- All repository pre-commit hooks pass on the exact source commit, including Black, isort and autoflake.\n\n## Local collation measurements\n\nTorch 2.9.1+cpu, Python 3.12, one hosted CPU runner with two threads. Each case has a 100-call warmup and seven timed blocks of 100 calls. Values below are medians per call; output SHA-256 values match before and after.\n\n| Batch × padded length | Before → after time | Before → after operator-allocated bytes |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 × 8,192 | 4.549 → 2.113 µs | 131,072 → 65,536 |\n| 8 × 8,192 | 37.835 → 35.926 µs | 1,048,576 → 1,048,576 |\n| 32 × 8,192 | 145.041 → 142.895 µs | 4,194,304 → 4,194,304 |\n| 32 × 8,192, varying lengths | 636.197 → 630.439 µs | 4,194,304 → 4,194,304 |\n\nThe measured improvement is the single-input collation path. Multi-input timing differences are controls for the unchanged algorithm. Allocation figures are positive operator allocation totals from the CPU profiler, not process peak memory. GPU, distributed training and end-to-end RL throughput were not measured.\n\nNative tests and benchmark: https://github.com/Afloat16/OpenRLHF/actions/runs/43422837888\n\nExact-source repository hooks: https://github.com/Afloat16/OpenRLHF/actions/runs/43422903546\n",
